<Base
  title="about — tank"
  description="shwetank shrey — engineer building end-to-end systems across hardware, firmware, mobile, and cloud."
>
  <article class="long-prose">
    <h1>about</h1>

    <p>
      i'm an engineer who builds end-to-end systems. the through-line
      across the work has been the data architecture — how information
      moves between hardware, firmware, mobile, and cloud without
      dropping or lying along the way. i've gone deep into each of those
      layers at different points, and worn the hats of a software
      engineer, an embedded engineer, a product manager, and a founder
      along the way.
    </p>
    <p>
      shwetank is the full name; <span class="accent">tank</span> came
      from the last four letters of it back in school. no big story
      behind it — it just stuck, and at some point i started owning it.
    </p>

    <h2>work</h2>
    <p>
      i co-founded tweek labs out of college. we built a wearable
      motion-capture suit<a href="#sn-mocap" class="fn" id="fnref-mocap">1</a>
      and the analytics platform behind it — used by professional sports
      teams. acquired by ultrahuman in 2024.
    </p>
    <Sidenote n="1">
      <span id="sn-mocap">imagine a camera following you everywhere you
      go, capturing every movement of your body in precise detail. that's
      what the suit does — except it does it without the camera, from
      sensors across the body.</span>
    </Sidenote>
    <p>
      at ultrahuman i kept the platform running for a while, then
      switched to a new project: a wrist wearable that picks up
      muscle-level signals from the skin<a href="#sn-emg" class="fn" id="fnref-emg">2</a>
      — quieter biology than what consumer wrist wearables usually try
      to measure. five hardware iterations, taken to pilot production.
    </p>
    <Sidenote n="2">
      <span id="sn-emg">the technical term is electromyography. it picks
      up the electrical activity of muscle contractions through the
      skin. usually done with sticky electrodes in a lab; making it
      stable on a moving wrist is the hard part.</span>
    </Sidenote>

    <p class="meta-line">
      <a href="/shwetank-shrey-resume.pdf">résumé</a> — last updated may 2026.
    </p>

    <h2>where i work from</h2>
    <p>
      i was born in patna and grew up between patna and mumbai. went to
      delhi for college, then worked between delhi and bangalore after
      that. settled in bangalore now.
    </p>
    <p>
      the last year has been different. i've spent more than a third of
      it in shenzhen, dealing with vendors. took a while to get used to
      the food — eventually i grew to like it. and when i could get out
      of the factories, i traveled around south china. it's extremely
      beautiful.
    </p>

    <h2>music</h2>
    <p>
      there should always be music running. i think life is better with
      a soundtrack, so i keep one going most of the day — in my
      earphones, or in my head. open-ear headphones (shokz openrun)
      help; i'm in the world and quietly scoring it at the same time.
    </p>
    <p>
      when i'm doing something that needs precision — mechanical
      assembly, two pieces that have to mate exactly — i play the
      docking scene from interstellar in my head. the assembly just
      happens, perfectly.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
